course: dictionary
lesson: 15338
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one you need. «The gardener laid a sod of grass over the bare soil.» The words grass, soil and laid tell you that sod means a piece of grass with the earth and roots under it. This is also called turf.
explain: Look for clues. A gardener lays turf on the ground, so the clues point to the garden meaning. Ask yourself: what would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Some uses of sod are rude slang. You may hear them in films or from angry grown-ups. Knowing what a word means helps you understand what someone says, but you should never use rude words at school.
fact: Sod means a piece of grass with the earth and roots under it. Another word for it is turf.
fact: «The first sod was turned at the new park.» Builders and gardeners use sods to make new lawns.
fact: Sod is also rude slang in the UK, Ireland and Australia. It is mild swearing, so do not use it at school.
fact: «Sod's law» is an informal saying. It means that if something can go wrong, it will.
fact: Context clues, such as the words around a word, help you tell which meaning it has.
exercise: «The workers laid fresh sods to make the football field green again.» What is a sod in this sentence? | *A piece of grass with earth and roots under it | A small wooden fence post | A ball kicked high into the air | A kind of football boot
exercise: «The first sod was turned at the new school today.» Which clue shows that sod is about digging the ground? | The word today | The word first | The word school | *The word turned, as in turning over soil
exercise: Ella hears a grown-up say «sod it» when the bus is late again. What does this show? | *The grown-up is fed up and giving up | The grown-up is asking for a sandwich | The grown-up is planting grass | The grown-up is very happy and laughing
test: What does «Sod's law» mean? | A rule about gardens | *If something can go wrong, it will | Everyone must be kind to each other | Grass grows fastest in the spring
test: Why should you not use the rude meanings of sod at school? | *Because they are rude swearing, even if you know what they mean | Because sod always means grass in school | Because they are too long to say | Because they are only used in Scotland
exam: Which sentence uses sod to mean a piece of grass? | *«We rolled out a sod to mend the lawn.» | «She read a big sod of books.» | «The sod was loud and bright.» | «The sod ran fast to school.»
